  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Sunday) \nIssued at 1022 PM CDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\n*** PROLONGED HOT AND DRY CONDITIONS ***\n\nThe upper high will take full control of the region as it noses into \nthe central and southern High Plains on Tuesday. As this happens, \nany rain chances will evaporate as temperatures soar. The mid-level \nhigh is progged to become centered squarely over the Texas Panhandle \nand Red River Valley by mid-week, with a 5960+ m 500 mb height \nprojected. The high will linger over the southern High Plains \nthrough late week, though it may eventually be nudged westward in \nresponse to a couple of shortwaves tracking over the top of the \nridge. As this occurs, the center of the ridge may shift toward the \nFour Corners this weekend. Depending how far west the ridge shifts, \nWest Texas could potentially find itself under weak northerly flow \non the east side of the ridge, though the ECMWF is less encouraging, \nmaintaining the eastern extent of the ridge over northwest Texas \nuntil late weekend. Should the eastern flank of the ridge breakdown \nquicker, it would open the door for recycled monsoon moisture and \nperhaps a weak front to edge into the region, providing minor \ncooling and a chance of thunderstorms as early as Friday evening.\n\nRegardless, until then the heat will be the theme through at least \nFriday. The current forecast shows triple digit highs (100-103 \ndegrees) off the Caprock starting Tuesday afternoon, increasing to \nnear or above 105 degrees there by Thursday and Friday. On the \nCaprock the trend will be the same, though about 3-6 degrees \n\"cooler\" depending on the location/elevation. The current forecast\ntemperatures could even be a couple of degrees too low if the \n30-35C 850 mb temperatures progged by some medium range guidance \ncomes to fruition. \n\nLow temperatures will follow a similar upward trend to the daytime \nhighs, residing mostly in the 70s, with even a few low 80s possible \noff the Caprock in the later half of the week. The combination of \nhot days and warm nights will be dangerous for those without access \nto cooling and proper hydration. Heat-related highlights are likely \nfor a good chunk of the FA later this week, potentially lingering \ninto the weekend if the upper ridge is slow to ease westward.",
